<Base title="About"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Yes, the weird versions are staying</p><h1>About the project</h1><p class="lead">They Forgot the Alamo is a low-poly browser game about a man who wakes up convinced he's Davy Crockett. This is where I'm keeping track of how it took shape and what still lies ahead.</p></header><section class="section"><h2>How it's being made</h2><p>This is my project: I set the direction, decide what I want, test builds, react to what I see, and ask for changes. AI coding tools do much of the coding and technical implementation. The shotgun has received an unreasonable number of notes about how Davy should hold it.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The current build</h2><p>The current desktop campaign runs through five chapters, from the hospital through the roadside evacuation, museum, and service tunnels to a final mission with two possible endings. It includes combat, chapter checkpoints, persistent clues, a consequential museum encounter, and hazards the raccoon can help shut down.</p><p>The immediate goal was a working desktop version. The larger wish list remains open: more detailed gore and body-part effects, a fuller soundscape, optional chapter content, and more elaborate crash and finale staging. Those additions are deferred, not forgotten.</p><p>The notes are based on commits, screenshots, model tests, and other project records. When an old version is rebuilt to get a screenshot, the archive says so. The oldest saved game version I can verify is from September 29, 2026. That's the oldest receipt I have, not necessarily the day I first started thinking about the game.</p></section></Base>
